**1) Dups sheet: Email, Name from latest row, Count, Latest Timestamp**

Put this in `A1` of `Dups`, replacing the earlier QUERY:

```
=IFERROR(LET(
  k, Sheet1!K2:K,
  d, FILTER({k, Sheet1!B2:B, Sheet1!I2:I, ROW(k)}, k<>"", ISNUMBER(Sheet1!I2:I)),
  L, SORTN(SORT(d, 3, FALSE, 4, FALSE), 9^9, 2, 1, TRUE),
  n, MAP(INDEX(L,,1), LAMBDA(x, COUNTIF(k, x))),
  {{"Email","Name (latest row)","Count","Latest Timestamp"};
   SORT(FILTER({INDEX(L,,1), INDEX(L,,2), n, INDEX(L,,3)}, n>1), 4, FALSE)}
), "No duplicated emails found")
```

- **How it works:** `SORT` orders rows by timestamp (newest first), then by sheet row (lowest on the sheet first) to break ties. `SORTN(..., 2, 1)` then keeps the first row per email, so the name comes from the same row your `SUPERSEDED` logic keeps.
- **Count:** `COUNTIF` counts every row with that email, including rows whose timestamp is `REVIEW`. An email whose rows all have unparsed timestamps won't appear at all.
- **Format:** Set the Latest Timestamp column to **Format > Number > Date time**.
- **Spill space:** It needs 4 empty columns and clear rows below `A1`.
- **Sort order:** It sorts by latest timestamp. For count instead, change `SORT(..., 4, FALSE)` to `SORT(..., 3, FALSE)`.

**2) Apps Script: copy kept, OK rows to `Final`**

```javascript
function exportFinal() {
  const ss = SpreadsheetApp.getActive();
  const src = ss.getSheetByName('Sheet1');
  const out = ss.getSheetByName('Final') || ss.insertSheet('Final');
  const WIDTH = 16;      // columns A:P
  const PHONE_COL = 8;   // H: cleaned phone, forced to plain text
  const last = src.getLastRow();
  if (last < 1) return;

  const range = src.getRange(1, 1, last, WIDTH);
  const values = range.getValues();
  const formats = range.getNumberFormats();

  // Row 0 is the header; O is index 14, P is index 15
  const keep = [0];
  for (let i = 1; i < values.length; i++) {
    if (values[i][14] !== 'SUPERSEDED' && values[i][15] === 'OK') keep.push(i);
  }

  out.clear();
  const target = out.getRange(1, 1, keep.length, WIDTH);
  target.setNumberFormats(keep.map(i => formats[i]));
  out.getRange(1, PHONE_COL, keep.length, 1).setNumberFormat('@');
  target.setValues(keep.map(i => values[i]));
}
```

- **Overwrite:** `out.clear()` wipes `Final` (contents and formatting) on every run, and the sheet is created if it doesn't exist.
- **Values only:** `Final` gets static values, not formulas, so re-run the script after new responses or edits.
- **Phones:** The phone column is set to plain text before writing, so Sheets doesn't reparse the 10-digit strings as numbers.
- **Columns:** It copies A:P, so raw and helper columns come along. To export only the cleaned columns, build each row from specific indexes, e.g. `keep.map(i => [values[i][1], values[i][10], values[i][6], values[i][7], values[i][8], values[i][9]])`, and adjust the range width and header to match.
- **Sanity check:** Compare the row count in `Final` (minus the header) with `=COUNTIFS(Sheet1!P2:P,"OK",Sheet1!O2:O,"<>SUPERSEDED")`. They should match.
